WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.760
And ai just turned one of math's toughest puzzles into a computer verified proof the size of a small library. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 157. Picture a homework problem so stubborn that people spent 350 years trying to finish it. That puzzle is for Matt's last theorem. A famous math challenge, a human mathematician finally solved in 1995. Now an artificial intelligence system, or ai, named Claude,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:57.880
has done something new with that old victory. It turned the whole proof into a version a computer can check line by line, with no skipped steps. A proof in plain language is a step by step argument that shows a math claim is definitely true. Think of it like turning a long hand-written recipe into a perfectly organized digital checklist. A robot can scan that checklist for any missing step or wrong measurement, and it will not get bored.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:27.340
The result is over 13 million lines of computer code. Along the way, the system also proved more than 29,000 extra theorems. The theorems are just math statements that have been shown to be true, like trustworthy building blocks. Those extra blocks had never been checked this carefully by a machine before. Here is why that matters far outside a math classroom. Math sits under almost everything you already love, from video games to medical research.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:54.200
When old proofs are this carefully verified, students and scientists can stack new ideas on top. They do not have to worry that some forgotten mistake will trip them up years later. Claude is acting like a super-patient proofreader that never gets tired of tiny details. For you, that could mean future textbooks or homework tools with machine-checked answers. Tough subjects get easier to learn when the foundation is locked down this tightly.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:14.520
Careers in science, engineering, and even game design may start relying on these verified blocks. You cannot run that giant proof on your own laptop right now, and that is okay. You can still try a tiny version of the same idea, for free, with no coding. Go to the free Lean Game website and open the beginner tutorials.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:33.380
Lean is a program that checks math the way a referee checks every move in a game. You prove simple statements step by step, and it gives you instant feedback, like a logic puzzle that talks back. That checking habit is the real magic, so let's slow down and see how an AI actually builds a proof like that.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:43.640
You already know the feeling of texting a friend and watching your phone guess the next word. Stretch that same idea across an entire math argument, not just one sentence.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:53.800
The AI is not tossing out random words. It has learned patterns from millions of earlier proofs. The way a friend who knows your texting style can finish your sentence.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:10.420
Each suggested step then hits a wall of strict rules. Picture a teacher who refuses any answer that skips even one line of work. If the step passes every rule, the AI keeps going. If it fails, the system backtracks and tries a different path.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:21.580
That is the same move you make when you erase a wrong turn in a strategy game and try again. Over millions of those tiny checks, the giant proof gets built and verified at the same time.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:33.360
The surprising part is the side work. The AI is also proving thousands of smaller facts it needs along the way. Many of those facts had never been written down in a form a machine could check.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:44.220
So the final result is not one big answer. It is a whole library of smaller, trustworthy facts that anyone can reuse later. That is why the file is so huge.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:51.220
The system is not only solving one puzzle, it is drawing a verified map of all the side roads that lead to the answer.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:59.560
Once you see it that way, a 13 million line proof stops feeling like a monster and starts feeling like a very careful packing list.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.960
Math can wait on the desk for a minute because a legendary instrument company just handed musicians a new toy.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:17.360
Roland, the company behind many famous keyboards and drum machines, released a plugin called Melody Flip. A plugin is just an add-on that drops into the music software you already use.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:28.060
Melody Flip gives you themed collections of musical ideas sorted by genre. You start with a short phrase, then let the AI suggest variations that still fit your style.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:37.280
You do not type a full song prompt, you pick a palette like grabbing a set of colors before you paint, and the tool flips short ideas into new ones.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:47.680
It keeps the same mood or rhythm so the new version still sounds like you. This is perfect if you already mess around with free digital audio workstations on a laptop or phone.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:54.680
A digital audio workstation or DAW is simply the app you use to record and mix music.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:01.320
If you have GarageBand, which is free on Apple devices, you can load the plugin and experiment right away.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:12.080
Here is a concrete challenge. Open GarageBand, load a simple drum loop, then ask Melody Flip to flip a two-bar melody into three new versions in the same style.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:18.340
Listen for which one sparks your next idea. The way a new Lego piece suddenly makes the whole build click.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.560
People are also noticing quieter changes when they use chat bots in daily life, not just for school.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:35.360
Some users say treating chat GPT like a strategic life advisor has helped with relationships, money decisions, health habits, and even understanding their own behavior.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.740
They describe it as an always available thinking partner, more like a coach on the bench than a homework machine.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:49.500
That is a different story from the usual, write my essay headlines because the focus is personal growth, not a quick answer.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.820
If you are curious, you can try a small version yourself for free.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:06:00.520
Open a free chat with chat GPT or with Gemini, another AI chat bot you can use in a browser.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:07.640
Type this. Help me think through a small goal I have this month, like improving my sleep or saving money for a game.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.520
Keep the conversation going over a few days and notice what actually changes.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.400
Treat it like a notebook that talks back, not like an oracle that cannot be wrong.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:25.700
While we are on everyday life, a lot of teenagers are already turning to chat bots when they need someone to listen, especially late at night.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:33.540
That can feel comforting, and it also means parents and teens need a plan for using these tools safely while still getting support.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:41.120
If you use a chat bot about feelings, remember it is software, not a substitute for a trusted person in the real world.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.260
One more thing people get wrong about the mood out there.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:52.940
A long time AI writer noticed that the same person can feel excited to try new tools and worried about bigger impacts at the same time.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:07:03.140
Online arguments often make it sound like everyone is all in or totally against AI, but real conversations are more mixed, and that mix is honest.

